 epicHistory: `
There were seven houses that signed the Binding, and ours was the seventh: House Viridane. The histories, burned and rewritten by the six who broke and then crowned House Morrath to hide the gap, will tell you otherwise, but the groves remember. The groves always remember.

House Viridane was the youngest of the noble families, the last to be elevated before the sun began its slow, final dimming. Their seat was in the Frostwood Reach, among the ancient ironwood forests that had stood since before the first human set foot on the continent. When Sol entered its Deepening and Keth-Amar descended to devour the dying star, all seven houses — Viridane among them — went to the Warden and agreed to the Binding: Sol would be entombed beneath the earth, its heat released through volcanic fissures, the price paid in the willing sacrifice of Aex, Sol's firstborn, whose living hide was woven into the seal. Viridane signed that bargain. All seven did, and for a time the Binding held.

Then came the Corruption Years. For eight years Keth-Amar whispered to the houses from beyond the vault, offering to crack the seal and free some of Sol's warmth — and demanding a steeper price: the firstborn heirs of every noble bloodline, marched to the northern peaks to be consumed as vessel-keys. Six of the seven houses broke and agreed. Viridane did not.

House Viridane refused the heir-sacrifice. Not publicly at first — they attended the councils, voted their dissent, and then, on the night before the march to the peaks, gathered every child, every servant, every sworn knight who would follow them, and fled south through the Frostwood Reach into the moonlit fae groves beyond. They were not pursued by Keth-Amar's frost-hounds. They were pursued by something worse: the knowledge that the seven families would never forgive the refusal, and that survival would require a counter-bargain of equal weight.

In the moonlit groves, ancient hollows where the protective fog thins and the old laws still hold, they found the fae. The fae offered protection, but the terms were absolute: wear your word in your flesh, let the grove anchor your memories against the mist, and never write down what you wish to preserve. The Briaran accepted. The thorns grew.

The seven families, meanwhile, began the erasure. It was not enough that Viridane had refused, the refusal had to be made impossible to remember. Three generations of dedicated arcanists scoured the name from every book, every treaty, every genealogical scroll. The Viridane sigils were pried from stone lintels. The Viridane dead were exhumed and reburied under blank markers. The Viridane living were declared a myth, a fever-dream, a lie told by peasants who had seen thorns on a stranger's arm and invented a noble house to explain them.

The story the seven families seeded, and that has seeped into every kingdom's understanding, is that House Viridane's cowardice weakened Keth-Amar's bargain, and the world's slow death by the Sun-Eater's hunger is their fault. This is the Oathbreaker's Stain: a lie told so often and so thoroughly that it has become the accepted history of every culture that trades with the old noble houses. The Skald believe it. The Emberth have been taught it. The Mimir, who know what it means to be erased, are the only race that openly doubts it, and even they are not sure.

The Briaran have survived for eight centuries in the spaces between official records. They do not appear in census counts. They do not appear in trade agreements. They are occasionally documented by scholars who later recant, by explorers who later vanish, by bounty hunters who later decide, inexplicably, to retire. The Trueborn hold the deep groves. the Shorn walk the wider world in borrowed names. Both carry the same wound: an ancestor abandoned during the flight south, a promise made to someone who was left behind and never seen again. Every Briaran bloodline still carries that promise. Every Briaran's thorns still drift toward it.

There is a fragment of the oral history that only the eldest elders pass down, not in the main recitation, but in whispers, after the children have slept, when the mist runs thick enough to hide secrets. It speaks of a pact that predated Keth-Amar's bargain. A moment, before the binding of Sol, when the seven families gathered not to save the world but to take from it. They hunted something in the dark above the world, a living radiance, a fire with purpose. The elders name it **Aex**, the firstborn of Sol, the guardian that had protected the sun through every Deepening since the first star learned to burn. Aex gave itself willingly — no coerced binding could hold a being of pure radiance — and House Solvan wielded the blade; its singing hide was woven into the seal, and the shards that now sleep beneath every continent are not stone but skin. House Viridane was present at that hunt. The elders do not know whether our ancestors participated or refused. The oral history is silent on that detail, or perhaps the detail was never recovered from the memory that the fog took. The thorns of our oldest dead still cluster toward that moment, unspeakable and unresolved.`,


